{
  "decision": "REVISE",
  "detail": {
    "days": 14,
    "checkpoint": "You can solve and explain one changed simple series circuit with Ohm's law, independently."
  },
  "plans": [
    {
      "planRef": "plan_1",
      "blueprint": {
        "schemaVersion": 2,
        "strategy": {
          "policy": "EXAM",
          "outcome": "Explain and use Ohm's law for one simple series circuit. Stop once I can solve and explain one changed circuit independently.",
          "explanation": "The accepted outcome is deliberately small: one simple series circuit, solved and explained independently. No prior completion or readiness evidence exists, so one integrated learning-and-application activity is the smallest useful step. It teaches the meaning of voltage, current and resistance, the relation V = IR and its rearrangements, how current is the same everywhere in a series loop and how source voltage divides across series resistors, then applies all of that to a supplied worked example and one changed circuit. Explanation and independent solving are combined because the outcome requires both and the topic is small enough that separating them would add sessions without adding evidence. No separate drill set, timed paper or second check is justified: the changed circuit is the independent evidence, and further practice is left for an observed failure.",
          "recommendedEffort": {
            "lowMinutes": 35,
            "likelyMinutes": 50,
            "highMinutes": 75
          },
          "phases": [
            {
              "localId": "phase_learn_apply",
              "title": "Learn and apply Ohm's law in one series circuit",
              "outcome": "Explain voltage, current and resistance in a simple series circuit and solve one changed circuit with V = IR, independently.",
              "effort": {
                "lowMinutes": 35,
                "likelyMinutes": 50,
                "highMinutes": 75
              },
              "readinessCriterion": "You correctly compute the requested quantity in the changed circuit and justify each step using V = IR and the series properties, without help."
            }
          ]
        },
        "scope": [
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_1",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "The whole accepted topic: Ohm's law and its use in one simple series circuit, taught and applied in a single integrated activity."
          }
        ],
        "activities": [
          {
            "localId": "ohm_series_circuit",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "phase_learn_apply"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1"
            ],
            "title": "Learn and apply Ohm's law to one simple series circuit",
            "activityType": "Integrated explanation and guided-to-independent problem solving",
            "purpose": "Build the needed concepts of voltage, current, resistance and series behaviour, then prove independent use of V = IR on a changed circuit.",
            "instructions": "Work through this in one sitting; write your reasoning as you go.\n\n1) Concepts. In a circuit, voltage (V, volts) is the electrical push across a component or source, current (I, amperes) is the rate of charge flow through it, and resistance (R, ohms) is how much it opposes that flow. Ohm's law relates them for a resistor: V = I x R, equivalently I = V / R and R = V / I. In a simple series circuit there is one path, so the same current flows through every component, and the source voltage is shared among the series resistors (the voltage drops add up to the source voltage).\n\n2) Worked example. A 12 V source is connected to two resistors in series, R1 = 30 ohm and R2 = 10 ohm. Total resistance: R_total = 30 + 10 = 40 ohm. Current: I = V / R_total = 12 / 40 = 0.3 A, the same through both resistors. Voltage across R1: V1 = I x R1 = 0.3 x 30 = 9 V. Voltage across R2: V2 = I x R2 = 0.3 x 10 = 3 V. Check: 9 + 3 = 12 V, matching the source. Notice which rearrangement of V = IR was used at each step.\n\n3) Your changed circuit. A 9 V source is connected to two resistors in series, R1 = 20 ohm and R2 = 40 ohm. On your own, with no help: (a) find the total resistance, (b) find the current through the circuit, (c) find the voltage across each resistor, and (d) check that the two voltage drops add to the source voltage. For each part, state in one sentence why the formula or series property you used applies.\n\n4) Explanation. In 3-5 sentences, explain to someone who has not studied this what current does in a series loop and why the source voltage splits across the resistors. Use your own numbers from part 3 as the example.\n\nIf you get stuck, re-read step 1 and the worked example and try again before looking anything up; if you still cannot proceed, note exactly where you stopped and treat that as the gap to address next.",
            "completionCriterion": "You have written correct answers for (a)-(d) of the 9 V circuit: R_total = 60 ohm, I = 0.15 A, V1 = 3 V, V2 = 6 V, with the check 3 + 6 = 9 V; each part is justified by V = IR or a series property; and your explanation correctly states that current is the same throughout the series loop and that the source voltage divides between the resistors. All done without assistance.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 35,
              "likelyMinutes": 50,
              "highMinutes": 75
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 60,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": [],
            "splitPoints": [
              {
                "afterMinutes": 25,
                "completionCriterion": "Concepts understood and the 12 V worked example fully solved and checked (current 0.3 A, V1 = 9 V, V2 = 3 V)."
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "decisions": {
          "assumptions": [],
          "tradeOffs": [
            "Only one integrated activity is booked. Extra practice sets, a timed paper and a second check are deliberately not scheduled because the changed circuit already provides the independent evidence the outcome requires; adding them would spend capacity without adding a distinct evidence contribution.",
            "The activity is treated as resumable at 25 minutes so it can fit around the preferred 30-minute chunk if needed; if it is completed in one sitting, the split point is simply not used."
          ],
          "unresolvedUncertainty": [
            "No prior completion or readiness evidence exists, so the effort estimate is a first-pass judgment; if the changed circuit is not solved correctly, the gap will be addressed then rather than pre-emptively."
          ],
          "stoppingConditions": [
            "Stop when the changed 9 V circuit is solved correctly and explained in your own words without assistance; do not continue to further circuits unless an error shows a specific gap."
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "sharedDependencies": [],
  "tradeOffExplanation": "The accepted outcome is one small, self-contained skill, so the plan invests in a single integrated learn-and-apply activity rather than splitting explanation, practice and checking into separate sessions. The changed circuit is the independent evidence; additional drills or a second verification pass would add time without adding a new kind of evidence and are therefore omitted. The only sacrifice is breadth: no parallel circuits, no multi-loop analysis and no timed exam technique are included, because none are part of the accepted outcome."
}

