              "localId": "diagnose",
              "title": "Series-circuit diagnostic",
              "outcome": "Evidence of whether the learner can compute total resistance, current, and voltage drops in a simple series circuit on fresh values, and correction of any specific error found.",
              "effort": {
                "lowMinutes": 20,
                "likelyMinutes": 30,
                "highMinutes": 45
              },
              "readinessCriterion": "All three computed values are correct on the fresh circuit without assistance; or any incorrect value is corrected and re-verified."
            }
          ]
        },
        "scope": [
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_1",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "The entire diagnostic and any error correction address simple series circuits: total resistance, current, and individual voltage drops."
          }
        ],
        "activities": [
          {
            "localId": "series_diagnostic",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "diagnose"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1"
            ],
            "title": "Short series-circuit diagnostic",
            "activityType": "Diagnostic problem set",
            "purpose": "Sample the reported competence on fresh values in one short sitting, and correct any specific error immediately, so the learner can stop early when performance is clean.",
            "instructions": "Use one simple series circuit with a DC source and three resistors in series. Choose your own fresh values, for example V = 12 V, R1 = 100 ohm, R2 = 220 ohm, R3 = 330 ohm (pick different numbers if you prefer). Work on paper and do the following in order:\n1. State that the same current flows through every element because the resistors are in series.\n2. Compute the total resistance R_total = R1 + R2 + R3.\n3. Compute the current I = V / R_total.\n4. Compute each voltage drop V1 = I*R1, V2 = I*R2, V3 = I*R3.\n5. Check that V1 + V2 + V3 equals the source voltage V; if it does not, find and fix the arithmetic error.\nDo this without help. If any value is wrong, identify the exact step where it went wrong, correct it, and re-check only that value. Do not repeat the whole set if it is already correct. When finished, you are done: there is no further committed work unless a specific error remains unresolved.",
            "completionCriterion": "All three computed values (R_total, I, and the three voltage drops) are correct on the fresh circuit without assistance, and V1 + V2 + V3 equals V; or any incorrect value is traced to its exact step, corrected, and re-verified. A clean diagnostic ends the plan.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 20,
              "likelyMinutes": 30,
              "highMinutes": 45
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 30,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": [],
            "stoppingCondition": "Stop once all computed values are correct and their sum checks against the source voltage, or once any error has been corrected and re-verified."
          }
        ],
        "decisions": {
          "assumptions": [
            "The learner has already been taught Ohm's law and the series-circuit rules (same current everywhere, resistances add, voltage drops sum to the source voltage), since the stated outcome is solving simple series circuits."
          ],
          "tradeOffs": [
            "No separate relearning or extended practice set is committed because the learner reports usually solving these correctly and no failure has been observed; committing more would exceed the accepted goal."
          ],
          "unresolvedUncertainty": [
            "No verified assessment weighting or recent performance evidence exists, so the diagnostic result itself is the only evidence used."
          ],
          "stoppingConditions": [
            "A clean diagnostic ends the plan; further work is contingent on a specific observed error, which is handled inside the diagnostic rather than as new committed work."
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "sharedDependencies": [],
  "tradeOffExplanation": "The accepted outcome asks only for a short diagnostic if useful and explicitly warns against certifying mastery from confidence. I therefore commit a single 30-minute diagnostic on fresh series-circuit values that also corrects any specific error found, and I do not commit separate relearning, a practice set, or a second full check. This keeps the plan small and lets the learner stop early when performance is clean, while still producing real evidence rather than relying on the learner's confidence."
}

