{
  "decision": "REVISE",
  "detail": {
    "days": 14,
    "checkpoint": "A working first CSV expenses report that reads a CSV with the csv module, groups rows by category, totals amounts, and flags malformed rows, demonstrated on a small file you create."
  },
  "plans": [
    {
      "planRef": "plan_1",
      "blueprint": {
        "schemaVersion": 2,
        "strategy": {
          "policy": "ROADMAP",
          "outcome": "Build a small CSV expenses report in Python that groups by category, reports totals, and checks malformed rows. I have never read a CSV in Python.",
          "explanation": "You have no prior CSV-reading experience, so the useful next checkpoint is a single small working report that exercises all three requested capabilities at beginner level: reading rows with the standard csv module, grouping and summing by category, and detecting/reporting malformed rows. Rather than separate technique drills, one integrated build on a small self-created data file lets you learn the constructs in context and produces a genuine artifact. Later engineering concerns (argparse interfaces, pandas, large-file streaming, richer validation) stay strategic and unbooked until this checkpoint works and is demonstrated.",
          "recommendedEffort": {
            "lowMinutes": 90,
            "likelyMinutes": 150,
            "highMinutes": 240
          },
          "phases": [
            {
              "localId": "checkpoint",
              "title": "First working expenses report",
              "outcome": "A runnable script that reads a small expenses CSV, groups by category with totals, and flags malformed rows, verified on your own sample data.",
              "effort": {
                "lowMinutes": 90,
                "likelyMinutes": 150,
                "highMinutes": 240
              },
              "readinessCriterion": "The script runs on your sample CSV and prints correct per-category totals while listing the malformed rows it detected."
            }
          ]
        },
        "scope": [
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_1",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Reading the expenses CSV with the standard csv module is the input step the report depends on and is exercised directly in the checkpoint activity."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_2",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Grouping rows by category and summing amounts is the core reporting logic built and verified in the checkpoint activity."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_3",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "Detecting and reporting malformed rows is implemented in the checkpoint activity so bad input is surfaced rather than silently summed."
          },
          {
            "scopeRef": "scope_4",
            "disposition": "INCLUDED",
            "contribution": "The small expenses report is the overall artifact the checkpoint activity produces and demonstrates."
          }
        ],
        "activities": [
          {
            "localId": "build_report",
            "outcomeRefs": [
              "checkpoint"
            ],
            "scopeRefs": [
              "scope_1",
              "scope_2",
              "scope_3",
              "scope_4"
            ],
            "title": "Build and verify a first CSV expenses report",
            "activityType": "Guided beginner build with self-check",
            "purpose": "Learn CSV reading, category grouping/totalling, and malformed-row handling by producing one small working report on your own sample data.",
            "instructions": "Work in a single Python file using only the standard library (csv module).\n\n1) Create your own small sample file expenses.csv with a header row category,amount and about 8-10 data rows, deliberately including 2-3 malformed rows (for example a missing amount, a non-numeric amount such as 'abc', or a row with too few fields). You are creating this data yourself; no sample is supplied.\n\n2) Open the file and read it with csv.reader (or csv.DictReader). Print the header and a few rows first so you can see what the reader returns, and note in comments what type each field is.\n\n3) Parse each data row: convert the amount field to a number. When a row cannot be parsed (missing/non-numeric amount, wrong number of fields), do not let the program crash: collect that row and a short reason into a list of malformed rows and skip it for totalling.\n\n4) Group the valid rows by category and sum the amounts, e.g. with a dict mapping category to running total.\n\n5) Print a report: one line per category with its total, and a final section listing each malformed row with its reason. Optionally print a grand total.\n\n6) Verify against your sample: hand-check that each category total matches the valid rows, and that every row you intended to be malformed appears in the malformed list. Fix any mismatch and re-run until the output is correct.\n\nKeep it small: a plain script with clear steps is enough. Do not add command-line arguments, pandas, or large-file handling yet; those are later strategic work.",
            "completionCriterion": "The script runs without crashing on your expenses.csv, prints correct per-category totals that match hand-checked sums of the valid rows, and lists every malformed row with a short reason; you can explain what csv.reader returns and why each flagged row is malformed.",
            "effort": {
              "lowMinutes": 90,
              "likelyMinutes": 150,
              "highMinutes": 240
            },
            "timeboxMinutes": 150,
            "commitment": {
              "category": "COMMITTED"
            },
            "hardDependencies": [],
            "splitPoints": [
              {
                "afterMinutes": 60,
                "completionCriterion": "expenses.csv exists and the script successfully prints the header and rows read via the csv module, with comments noting field types."
              },
              {
                "afterMinutes": 110,
                "completionCriterion": "Valid rows are parsed to numeric amounts and grouped by category with a first printed total per category; malformed rows are collected with reasons instead of crashing."
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "decisions": {
          "assumptions": [
            "The learner has basic Python syntax (variables, loops, dicts, functions) but no prior CSV-reading experience, as stated in the outcome."
          ],
          "tradeOffs": [
            "One integrated build is chosen over separate csv-reading, grouping, and validation drills because the requested outcome is a small report and the constructs are best learned together at beginner scale.",
            "Advanced topics (argparse, pandas, streaming large files, robust schema validation) are intentionally left unbooked to keep the first checkpoint achievable; they can be added once the basic report is demonstrated."
          ],
          "unresolvedUncertainty": [
            "The learner's exact prior Python fluency is unknown; if basic loops/dicts are not yet comfortable, the same activity may take longer within the stated high estimate."
          ],
          "stoppingConditions": [
            "Stop once the report runs correctly on the learner's own sample CSV and the malformed rows are correctly identified, even if the sample is small.",
            "Do not extend into command-line interfaces, pandas, or large-file handling in this checkpoint."
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "sharedDependencies": [],
  "tradeOffExplanation": "This plan commits a single integrated beginner activity instead of separate drills, because the accepted outcome is one small report and the three requested capabilities (CSV reading, grouping/totalling, malformed-row handling) are naturally learned together on a tiny self-created dataset. The cost is that later engineering concerns such as CLI arguments, pandas, or large-file streaming are not addressed now; that is deliberate, since they are not needed for the first useful checkpoint and would dilute a beginner build. The learner must create their own sample CSV because no data is supplied; this is part of the work, not a substitute for it."
}

