            "instructions": "The course note gives outcomes, not methods or sample data, so prepare both before building. (1) Choose beginner methods for the three outcomes and write one or two sentences for each explaining why it fits: for reading rows, a standard CSV reader that handles quoted fields (for example a quoted comma inside a field is valid data, not an error); for grouping, a simple dictionary or map that accumulates a running total per category; for malformed-row handling, a check that each row has the expected number of fields and a numeric amount, with a clear message for any row that fails. (2) Create a small expense CSV file yourself (about 8-12 rows) with columns for date, category, and amount, containing at least two categories and at least one deliberately malformed row (for example a missing amount or a non-numeric amount). Also note which single quoted field you will include to confirm quoted commas are read correctly. Do not use any method you cannot explain; if you are unsure, pick the simplest one that works.",
